EXPR(1)                        P32W Unix                         EXPR(1)

NAME
       expr - evaluate arguments as an expression

SYNOPSIS
       expr arg ...

DESCRIPTION
       expr evaluates the expression formed by its arguments and
       writes the result, followed by a newline, on the standard
       output. Each operand and operator must be a separate
       argument. Operands are strings; those made of digits with an
       optional leading - are integers, from -2147483648 to
       2147483647. A result is false if it is empty or an integer
       equal to 0 (0, 00, -0), true otherwise.

       The operators, from lowest precedence to highest (operators
       of equal precedence group from the left):
       a | b  a if it is true; otherwise b if it is not empty;
              otherwise 0
       a & b  a if both a and b are true, otherwise 0
       a = b, a != b, a < b, a <= b, a > b, a >= b
              1 if the comparison holds, otherwise 0; integers are
              compared as numbers, anything else as strings
       a + b, a - b
              sum, difference
       a * b, a / b, a % b
              product, quotient, remainder (truncated toward zero)
       a : re match a against the regular expression re, which is
              anchored at the start of a (as if it began with ^).
              The result is the text matched by the first \( \) in
              re, or the empty string if there is no match; if re
              has no \( \), the number of characters matched (0 if
              none)
       ( expr )
              grouping

       The regular expressions are the basic ones of ed(1) and
       grep(1):
       c      an ordinary character matches itself
       \c     c literally (for . * [ \ and $)
       .      any character
       [set]  any character in the set; a-z is a range, and a ]
              first is a member; [^set] any character not in it
       x*     zero or more of x (a character, ., a set); a * at
              the start is an ordinary character
       x\{m\}, x\{m,\}, x\{m,n\}
              m of x; at least m; m to n
       \( \)  a group, to report or to repeat with \1 ... \9
       $      at the end: the end of a
       ^      at the start: ignored (the match is anchored anyway)

       Many operators mean something to sh(1) and must be quoted:
       \*, '<', '>', '|', '&', \( and \).

EXAMPLES
       n=`expr $n + 1`              count
       expr 7 \* 6                  prints 42
       expr 17 % 5                  prints 2
       expr "$USER" = ken           prints 1 or 0
       expr "$NAME" \| default      a default for an empty value
       expr "$f" : '.*\.c$'         nonzero if $f ends in .c
       expr "$f" : '\(.*\)\.c$'     $f without its .c
       expr //"$p" : '.*/\(.*\)'    the last component of a path
       expr "$s" : '.*'             the length of $s

EXIT STATUS
       0 if the result is true; 1 if it is false (empty or 0); 2
       for an error, with a message: "expr: syntax error", "expr:
       not a number" (an arithmetic operand that is not an
       integer), "expr: integer overflow" (an operand or a result
       out of range), "expr: division by zero" or "expr: bad
       regular expression".

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), test(1), grep(1), ed(1)

BUGS
       There are no match, substr, index or length keywords (GNU);
       : does what match does, and expr "$s" : '.*' is the length.

       An operand that looks like an operator is taken as one: expr
       "$x" : y fails if $x is (. Prefix such strings, as in the
       example with //.

       Comparisons of strings use character codes. A \( \) cannot
       be followed by * or \{ \}.
